## Upstream data

The tables under `for_*/` are derived from the sources below. Where the source is
licensed CC BY 4.0, the credit line that licence requires is given with the entry.

### UniProt — CC BY 4.0
UniProt Consortium — https://www.uniprot.org

The enzyme, gene, GO, isoform, reference, sequence-link and solubility tables are
derived from UniProtKB (Swiss-Prot and TrEMBL) exports, retrieved with
`update_tool/download_uniprot.py` and reshaped by `update_tool/update_database.py`.

### Rhea — CC BY 4.0
SIB Swiss Institute of Bioinformatics / EMBL-EBI — https://www.rhea-db.org

Reaction equations, physiological direction, EC numbers and reaction SMILES are
derived from the Rhea SPARQL endpoint.

### ChEBI — CC BY 4.0
EMBL-EBI — https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi

SMILES and InChIKey mappings are derived from the ChEBI flat-file release.
`update_tool/chebi_data/curation_overrides.tsv` is our own manual curation layered
on top of it.

### DDBJ / INSDC — no use restrictions
https://www.ddbj.nig.ac.jp

Nucleotide sequence links are looked up through `getentry.ddbj.nig.ac.jp`. DDBJ and
the other INSDC partners state that they place no restrictions on the use or
redistribution of published nucleotide sequence data; DDBJ records explicitly that
there is no copyright restricting use or redistribution.

## Bundled third-party software

### Ketcher
`frontend/public/ketcher_standalone/` is a prebuilt Ketcher **v3.18.0** distribution
(EPAM Systems, Apache License 2.0 — https://github.com/epam/ketcher), used for
structure drawing and structure search. We ship it as received and modified nothing.

Three kinds of licence file travel with it, and they cover different things:

- **Ketcher's Apache License 2.0 text** — `ketcher_standalone/LICENSE` is EPAM's own
  copy, appendix and all. §4(a) requires a redistributor to give recipients a copy of
  the License.
- **Ketcher's own NOTICE** — reproduced verbatim at
  `frontend/public/ketcher_standalone/NOTICE`. §4(d) requires a redistributor to pass
  on the attribution notices of the upstream work. That file was **not** included in
  the prebuilt bundle we received; we fetched it from the EPAM repository and put it
  next to the copy we ship.
- **The bundled libraries** — the build's `*.LICENSE.txt` sidecars, kept alongside
  it, list the licences of the libraries webpack pulled in (lodash, React, Three.js,
  Miew, object-assign, buffer, crc32.js, regenerator-runtime,
  url-search-params-polyfill). These are *not* Ketcher's own licence text; Ketcher
  itself is covered by the repository's root [`LICENSE`](LICENSE), which is Apache
  License 2.0 — the same licence EPAM distributes it under.

Both Ketcher files were compared against upstream tag `v3.18.0` on 2026-10-05 and are
byte-for-byte identical to it. Keep the sidecars: each bundled JavaScript file ends
with a banner pointing at one of them, so deleting one leaves a dangling reference —
and they are not an exhaustive inventory of what is bundled. Details in
`docs/wiki/database-protocol.md` §12.4.

## Not redistributed

Two upstream components are absent from this repository, in two different senses. BLAST+ runs on
**your** machine and leaves nothing in what we ship. DeepSolNet ran once on **ours**, and its output
is part of the data we do ship — so it is recorded here even though nothing of it is redistributed.

### NCBI BLAST+
Sequence homology search runs a locally installed NCBI BLAST+ as a subprocess.

NCBI distributes BLAST+ under a **Public Domain Notice**: it is a "United States
Government Work", written as part of the authors' official duties, and therefore
**cannot be copyrighted** — the notice states that NLM and the U.S. Government "have
not placed any restriction on its use or reproduction". No licence or permission is
needed to use it. NCBI does ask that the authors be cited in work based on it, and
we do so here.

The executables are **not** in this repository; each user downloads the build for
their own platform — see `docs/wiki/database-protocol.md` §3.4. That page also tells
you to keep the `LICENSE` file that ships inside the NCBI archive: if you go on to
redistribute BLAST+ yourself, that file is the notice you need to pass along, and the
archive's copy of it — not a paraphrase — is the authoritative one.

### DeepSolNet / ESM C 300M
The per-enzyme solubility scores in
`for_enzyme_detail/child_tables/uniprotkb_solubility_score.*.tsv` were produced by
DeepSolNet (https://github.com/wangxinglong1990/DeepSolNet), which builds on ESM C 300M. **What we
ship from this is the scores, as data** — neither the tool nor the model weights are in this
repository, and no part of either is redistributed.

This is the one case where the model's name survives in the shipped artefact itself: the column is
called `DeepSolNet Score`. The loader (`etl/sources.py`, via `pandas.read_csv` with no comment
handling) would read a leading `#` block as data, so the provenance had to go into the column name
rather than a header comment. See `for_enzyme_detail/child_tables/solubility_scores.md`.

`docs/wiki/database-protocol.md` §12.2 records the current terms of both and the date they were
checked.

